Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from the short story by Edgar Allan Poe
Draft date: 5/1/2025
Notes: Photorealistic live-action. Period mid-19th-century domestic interiors. Closed cast tokens: NARRATOR, OLD MAN, OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER SHAW, OFFICER BLAKE. Neighbour remains off-screen.

FADE IN:

INT. THE OLD HOUSE - NIGHT

A cramped, aged interior of worn wood floors and plaster walls. NARRATOR, adult male of indeterminate age, face taut with nervous energy, eyes alert and listening, stands alone in muted earth-tone day clothes slightly rumpled. He addresses an unseen listener with forced calm.

(SOUND: dreadful silence of the old house, faint settling of timber)

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

He touches his ear, listening outward.

NARRATOR
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad?

(SOUND: distant low creak, as if from walls)

NARRATOR
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Pitch-black room. Shutters close fastened. A heavy wooden bed. OLD MAN, elderly, lies asleep in a plain unbleached linen nightshirt. NARRATOR in dark close-fitting night clothes, stockinged feet, holds a closed dark lantern of tin.

(SOUND: soft latch click, almost inaudible)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

NARRATOR eases the chamber door open a hand's width, thrusts the closed lantern in, then his own head, moving with exaggerated caution.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Morning light through shutters now ajar. NARRATOR, neat in muted earth-tone waistcoat, shirt and trousers, enters boldly. OLD MAN sits up in the heavy bed. NARRATOR speaks in a hearty tone, smiling.

(SOUND: floorboards under cheerful footsteps, morning birds faint beyond shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

NARRATOR
And how did you pass the night?

OLD MAN answers with a vague nod and murmur. NARRATOR clasps his shoulder kindly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Montage compression of seven nights. Each night: NARRATOR in dark stealth clothes turns the latch oh so gently. He opens a gap sufficient for his head. Inserts the closed dark lantern. Slowly—very, very slowly—thrusts his head in until he can see OLD MAN upon the bed. He undoes the lantern just enough that a single thin ray falls toward the pillow. The eye is always closed. He withdraws.

(SOUND: repeated soft latch, faint hinge creak of lantern, NARRATOR's controlled breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently! It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening. Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this? And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Eighth night. Room black as pitch, shutters fastened through fear of robbers. NARRATOR at the chamber door and latch, dark clothes, closed dark lantern in hand. His hand on the latch moves slower than a watch's minute hand. A faint triumphant smile. He chuckles under his breath.

(SOUND: almost no sound but the narrator's tiny chuckle and the latch easing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph.

OLD MAN stirs on the bed suddenly, as if startled. NARRATOR freezes, then keeps pushing the door open steadily, steadily. He has his head in, about to open the lantern, when his thumb slips upon the tin fastening.

(SOUND: sharp metallic slip of tin fastening)

OLD MAN
Who's there?

NARRATOR keeps quite still, says nothing. He does not move a muscle. OLD MAN sits up in the bed, listening in the dark.

(SOUND: thick silence, then the faint tick of death-watches in the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening;—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Still the same black room. NARRATOR remains motionless in the doorway gap, lantern closed. After a long time a slight groan rises from the bed.

(SOUND: low stifled groan of mortal terror)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well.

NARRATOR's face shows pity and a private chuckle. He waits with extreme patience, then opens a very, very little crevice in the lantern.

(SOUND: tiny creak of lantern hinges)

A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, shoots from the crevice and falls full upon the open eye—pale blue, dull, with a hideous veil.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones; but I could see nothing else of the old man's face or person.

(SOUND: low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton)

NARRATOR's eyes widen. He holds the lantern motionless, scarcely breathing. The sound grows quicker and louder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the senses?—now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart. It increased my fury.

The hellish tattoo grows louder. NARRATOR's face contorts with terror that a neighbour might hear.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder, quicker, filling the silence of the old house)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell NARRATOR throws open the lantern, leaps into the room. OLD MAN shrieks once—once only. NARRATOR drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: yell, single shriek, heavy bedframe scraping and thudding)

NARRATOR stands over the smothered form, smiling gaily. The muffled heart still beats for many minutes, then ceases. NARRATOR removes the bed, places a hand on the chest, waits.

(SOUND: muffled heartbeat fading to nothing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, he was stone, stone dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Night waning. NARRATOR works hastily but in silence by dim lantern light. He dismembers the corpse—head, arms, legs—clinical, no lingering. A tub catches all. He takes up three planks from the flooring, deposits the parts between the scantlings, replaces the boards so flush no eye could detect wrong. No stain left.

(SOUND: soft sawing and cutting, boards lifted and seated, tub scrape)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body. There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A bell sounds the hour of four. Still dark as midnight. A knocking at the street door.

(SOUND: distant bell striking four, firm knocking below)

INT. THE OLD HOUSE - STREET DOOR - NIGHT

NARRATOR, now in slightly rumpled day clothes, light of heart, goes down and opens the heavy street door. Three men enter: OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER SHAW, OFFICER BLAKE, in dark wool frock coats, caps removed, composed and suave.

(SOUND: door latch, night air, three pairs of boots on threshold)

NARRATOR
Gentlemen. Welcome.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour. Suspicion of foul play. We are to search the premises.

NARRATOR smiles.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

He bids them enter fully.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

NARRATOR leads OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER SHAW, and OFFICER BLAKE through the house and at length into the chamber. He shows the old man's treasures, secure, undisturbed. In the enthusiasm of confidence he brings chairs in. He places his own seat directly upon the spot beneath which the corpse reposes. The officers sit. They appear satisfied.

(SOUND: chairs set down, pleasant low conversation of familiar things)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was singularly at ease. They sat, and while I answered cheerily, they chatted of familiar things.

NARRATOR's face slowly loses color. He rubs his temple. A ringing begins.

(SOUND: faint ringing that grows more distinct)

NARRATOR talks more freely, voice heightening. The sound resolves into the low, dull, quick beat—like a watch enveloped in cotton. The officers chat on and smile, hearing nothing.

(SOUND: heartbeat returning, steady, under the officers' calm voices)

NARRATOR rises, argues about trifles with violent gesticulations. He paces the floor with heavy strides. He foams, raves, swears. He swings his chair and grates it upon the boards.

(SOUND: chair legs grating wood, heartbeat louder still, drowning the chat)

NARRATOR
(why will they not be gone?)

The noise grows louder—louder—louder. The officers continue to smile pleasantly.

(SOUND: heartbeat overwhelming, room almost vibrating)

NARRATOR
(shrieking)
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

He points wildly at the floorboards under his chair. The officers' smiles vanish as they stare at the spot.

(SOUND: final ringing heartbeat peak, then abrupt silence)

FADE OUT.

THE END